FlexNPU-AI-Hardware v0.1 — LICENSE (UNRESOLVED)

*** DO NOT PUBLISH THIS REPO WITHOUT REPLACING THIS FILE. ***

Canonical FlexNPU licensing is currently ambiguous. Evidence gathered
during the v0.1 dry run:

  * /home/yg9bq/Iree_workplace/FlexNPU has NO LICENSE, LICENSE.md, or
    COPYING file at any depth.
  * /home/yg9bq/Iree_workplace/FlexNPU/README.md contains exactly one
    "## License" section whose body reads "TBD."
  * No SPDX identifiers appear in any source file (Python, SystemVerilog,
    C++, YAML).
  * No copyright notices appear in file headers.

Because the derived student repo copies files verbatim from canonical
FlexNPU (see `README.md` §"What you get"), it CANNOT be published under
a permissive open-source license until the canonical maintainer commits
to one and applies it to the canonical repo.

Recommended resolution path:

  1. FlexNPU maintainer picks a permissive license (MIT, Apache-2.0,
     or BSD-3-Clause). MIT is the smallest-footprint choice; Apache-2.0
     is the safer choice if any patent claims are anticipated.
  2. Maintainer commits a LICENSE file to canonical FlexNPU and adds
     an SPDX-License-Identifier line to top-level source files (the
     RTL, compiler seam files, ctb_mech, and testbenches this repo
     borrows from).
  3. Replace THIS file with a copy of the same license text.
  4. Add an SPDX header to newly authored files in this repo:
       // SPDX-License-Identifier: <chosen>       (for .sv / .cpp)
       # SPDX-License-Identifier: <chosen>        (for .py / .yaml)
  5. Update `README.md` §"License" to name the chosen license.

Do NOT guess a license for the student repo. Publishing FlexNPU-derived
code under a license the canonical author has not endorsed would create
a licensing conflict downstream.
